# /opt/dripdrop/.env  —  the inboxslide.ai instance (Mike's new venture)
#
# ARENA RUNS ALONGSIDE THIS, on a different droplet, and keeps running.
# Nothing in this file may ever be copied onto Arena's box. Every variable
# here defaults to Arena's existing hardcoded behaviour, which is what keeps
# Arena correct -- but ONLY while they stay unset there. Setting
# DRIPDROP_ATS_EMAILS on Arena, for one, would revoke Pipeline access for
# Sarah Henze and Elizabeth Simonov, silently.
#
# Copy to /opt/dripdrop/.env on the new droplet (chmod 600, root-owned). Read by
# BOTH systemd units via EnvironmentFile=. See
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-15-whitelabel-instance-design.md
#
# Angle-bracket values are placeholders — every one must be replaced. Nothing
# here is copied from Arena's box except the file's shape: the API key, the
# OAuth apps and the session secret are all this instance's own.
#
# This is NOT a client white-label. This instance is where Mike keeps operating
# after leaving Arena, so every value that points back at Arena is a dependency
# that dies on his last day. That is why none of them may be left at default.

# ── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A key from Mike's OWN Anthropic organization, on his own card. NOT a key from
# a workspace inside Arena's org: workspace keys are bound to the user who made
# them and are DEACTIVATED the moment that user leaves the organization, so such
# a key would stop working on exactly the day this instance starts mattering.
# Create it with NO expiry, and set a monthly spend limit on the workspace.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<key from Mike's own Anthropic org>

# Session-cookie signing secret. Generate FRESH on the new droplet:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
# Never reuse Arena's — a shared secret means a cookie minted on one instance
# authenticates on the other. The app refuses to start if this is unset.
DRIPDROP_SECRET=<output of: openssl rand -hex 32>

# ── Per-instance identity (the six gates that were hardcoded to Arena) ───────
# Each of these defaults to Arena's value when unset, so every one must be set
# here explicitly. thrivemodal.com is the venture's email domain (partner-owned).

# The Pipeline (ATS) tab. This instance is sales-only -- no recruiting -- so
# it is switched off entirely.
#
# It cannot be switched off by blanking the two allowlists below. _env_list()
# falls back to its built-in default when a variable is unset OR BLANK
# (flowdrip_app.py:65-71), and that default includes Mike's gmail, so
# "DRIPDROP_ATS_EMAILS=" would have left Pipeline visible. Hence a real
# boolean. It is read in BOTH flowdrip_app.py (which gates the nav button) and
# ats.py (which gates the /ats page itself) -- gating only the button would
# leave the page reachable by typing the URL.
#
# Default is "1" = on, which is Arena's behaviour. Setting this on Arena's box
# would remove the Pipeline from Arena entirely.
DRIPDROP_ATS_ENABLED=0

# Left UNSET on purpose. With DRIPDROP_ATS_ENABLED=0 they have no effect, and
# setting them would be misleading about who can see a tab that does not exist
# here. If this instance ever does take on recruiting, delete the line above
# and set these -- by domain where possible, individually only for accounts
# outside it.
#DRIPDROP_ATS_DOMAINS=
#DRIPDROP_ATS_EMAILS=

# Recruiter addresses the résumé contact-extractor must NEVER mistake for the
# candidate's. Verified empirically 2026-09-15: left at the Arena default, a
# résumé sourced by dana.reed@thrivemodal.com returns *the recruiter's* address
# as the candidate's, and that recruiter gets enrolled into candidate outreach.
# Set correctly it returns the candidate. This is the one that actually bites.
#
# NEVER put a consumer domain here. "gmail.com" would match the operator's own
# address -- and also the personal address of most candidates, whose real email
# would then be dropped, silently, with no error and no way to tell afterwards
# which records lost one. Verified 2026-09-16.
DRIPDROP_INTERNAL_DOMAINS=thrivemodal.com

# Same job, matched on the FULL address instead of the domain. This exists for
# exactly the situation this instance is in: an operator working out of a
# consumer mailbox has no domain that can be safely skipped, so the operator is
# named outright. Every address that sources or forwards résumés belongs here.
# Empty by default, so it stays inert on Arena.
DRIPDROP_INTERNAL_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# The Roundup (gated internal newsletter). OWNER is not "who receives it" --
# it is the account whose per-user folder STORES every issue, and the identity
# the send worker runs as (flowdrip_app.py:25375, :26020). So whoever is named
# here, the newsletter sends FROM that account's connected mailbox.
#
# This is Mike's own venture, so unlike a client white-label there is no
# handover: he is the permanent owner and this stays his address. Make it the
# address he intends to KEEP, not a provisioning placeholder -- moving it later
# also moves where issues are STORED, so existing issues must be copied across
# user folders. Cheap now while the folder is empty, annoying once it isn't.
DRIPDROP_ROUNDUP_OWNER=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com
# Who may VIEW the Roundup tab. The owner is always included automatically.
DRIPDROP_ROUNDUP_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Owner of legacy pre-multi-user candidates/pipelines. Inert on this instance
# -- it starts with an empty data dir, so there are no legacy records to
# assign. Set anyway so the value is never Arena's if records ever appear.
DRIPDROP_OWNER_EMAIL=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Not one of the six (it was already env-driven), but it ALSO defaults to
# michael.vaughn@arenastaffing.net -- an address Mike loses on his last day at
# Arena. Unset, nobody on this box has the keys.
DRIPDROP_SUPER_ADMINS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# Permanent admin accounts, checked *in addition to* DRIPDROP_SUPER_ADMINS.
# Was a hardcoded pair of Arena addresses, which made them admins on every
# instance no matter what DRIPDROP_SUPER_ADMINS said. Must be set here.
DRIPDROP_ADMIN_EMAILS=mkvaughn1987@gmail.com

# ── Outbound identity: what leaves the box ──────────────────────────────────
# This instance's own public origin, no trailing slash. Every absolute URL in
# outgoing mail is built from it: hosted email images, password-reset links,
# newsletter deep links, the API example in onboarding. Left at the Arena
# default, this instance writes image files to its OWN disk and then links them
# on Arena's domain, so every image in this instance's mail 404s. Silently.
DRIPDROP_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://app.inboxslide.ai

# Company identity stamped on outgoing mail and on the Roundup page. This is the
# legal sending entity under CAN-SPAM, so it must be the venture's real
# registered name -- not a working title.
DRIPDROP_COMPANY_NAME=<the venture's registered company name>

# The CAN-SPAM postal footer on every Roundup issue. This is a legal
# requirement on commercial email and it must be the SENDING entity's own
# registered postal address. The default is Arena's street address -- shipping
# that misidentifies the sender on every issue this venture sends.
DRIPDROP_COMPANY_ADDRESS=<Venture Name | Street | City, ST ZIP US>

# Banner artwork stamped into every J's Way newsletter body. The bundled
# default is Arena-branded. Leave EMPTY to drop the image entirely; set it to
# an absolute URL only once the venture has artwork of its own served from this
# instance.
DRIPDROP_JWAY_BANNER_URL=

# ── Mailbox OAuth ───────────────────────────────────────────
# The DripDrop account is identified by an email address. That is a LOGIN
# identity and nothing more — mail is NOT sent from it by virtue of logging in.
# Sending comes from whatever mailbox is connected in that account's Settings
# (flowdrip_app.py:3031-3097), stored per-user, independent of the login.
#
# Gmail OAuth is LIVE on this instance and is the sender. Connecting it is an
# explicit act in Settings, not a side effect of signing in: the only Google
# callback in the app is /auth/google/callback (flowdrip_app.py:54391) and
# every branch that starts that flow sits in the Settings / mailbox region
# (:43797, :43998, :44346). The login page (:2427, :2471) has no Google
# button at all — registration here is email + password + invite code.
#
# (An earlier draft of this file claimed that signing in with Google and
# connecting Gmail-for-sending were the same grant, and told you to leave these
# unset. That was wrong. It is corrected here rather than deleted, because the
# same wrong warning was copied into docs/LAUNCH-inboxslide.md Step 7 and
# docs/HANDOFF-inboxslide.md — if you find it still there, it is stale.)
#
# What IS true: the send chain tries Microsoft Graph, then Gmail OAuth, then
# SMTP, and takes the FIRST one holding a token. So a connected Gmail outranks
# any SMTP settings. That is intended here — just do not also configure SMTP
# and expect it to be used.
#
# All three are SET on the server. GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI must be overridden
# because it defaults to Arena's host (gmail_oauth.py:35), and the callback
# registered in the Google Cloud console must match it EXACTLY.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<this instance's own OAuth client id>
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=<its secret — set on the server, never pasted into chat>
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=https://app.inboxslide.ai/auth/google/callback
#
# Two things that bite later:
#  - An app left in "Testing" has its refresh tokens expired by Google after
#    ~7 days, so the scheduled sender silently stops a week after the mailbox
#    connects. Publish it to "In production".
#  - Consent is per-user (gmail_oauth.py:48 — gmail.send, gmail.readonly,
#    openid/email/profile), so no Workspace admin and no domain owner is
#    involved; the Cloud project can live under any personal Google account.
#
# Rotating the secret does NOT disconnect an already-connected mailbox — the
# stored refresh token keeps working. /root/setgoogle.sh on the server takes a
# new secret invisibly and restarts the app.

# Microsoft Graph — deferred, optional. Nothing needs it while Gmail is the
# sender. If it is ever enabled it will outrank Gmail in the send chain above.
#MS_CLIENT_ID=
#MS_CLIENT_SECRET=
#MS_REDIRECT_URI=https://app.inboxslide.ai/auth/microsoft/callback
# Leave MS_TENANT_ID UNSET unless the Azure app is registered single-tenant.
# Unset means the MSAL authority is .../common, which is what Arena has always
# used and what a multi-tenant registration requires. Set it to the tenant id
# ONLY for a single-tenant registration -- /common rejects those with
# AADSTS50194. Until 2026-09-15 this variable was read and then ignored, so a
# single-tenant registration would have failed with no obvious cause.
#MS_TENANT_ID=

# ── Registration (NOT optional -- read this) ────────────────────────────────
# Gates account creation. Unset does NOT mean "closed": _load_invite_codes()
# falls back to a hardcoded default code that is the same in every build,
# including Arena's (flowdrip_app.py:2642-2647). Leaving this unset would let
# anyone holding Arena's invite code create an account on this instance.
#
# Set it to a fresh code of this instance's own. Anything unguessable is fine:
#     openssl rand -hex 4
DRIPDROP_INVITE_CODES=<a fresh code for this instance -- do NOT reuse Arena's>

# ── Sales claims (LAUNCH BLOCKER -- read before the first 4×4 send) ───────
# The "proven results" bullet list the 4×4 (and its 5×5 / 5×3 variants) assert
# to every prospect. The default is ARENA'S: an 80-90% fill rate, a 2-3 week
# fill time, and a replacement guarantee. Those are factual claims about a track
# record and about commercial terms. A brand-new venture has neither, so leaving
# this unset means this instance's first sales email makes claims it cannot
# support, under someone else's numbers.
#
# Set it to claims this venture can actually stand behind, or set it EMPTY to
# drop the section entirely. Empty is the honest default until there are numbers.
DRIPDROP_VALUE_PROPS=

# ── Brand and vocabulary ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The product name and the water-metaphor wording ("drip", "Slow Drip",
# "Today's Drip") are per-instance. Every variable below defaults to the exact
# wording the app has always shipped, so Arena — which sets none of them — is
# unchanged. DO NOT find-and-replace these words in the source: Arena runs the
# same flowdrip_app.py, and a rename there renames Arena's product inside
# Arena's own app. Defined at flowdrip_app.py:80-135.
#
# Quote any value containing an apostrophe. /opt/dripdrop/.env is read by BOTH
# systemd (EnvironmentFile=) and python-dotenv; a bare ' opens a quote to
# systemd and the line is rejected. Double quotes are safe for both.

DRIPDROP_BRAND_NAME=inboxslide
DRIPDROP_BRAND_WORDMARK=inboxslide
DRIPDROP_BRAND_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@inboxslide.ai

# Left UNSET until a real logo file is on the server. The default points at the
# bundled DripDrop artwork, so until this is set the old logo still shows. It
# must be a path this instance actually serves — setting it to a file that does
# not exist gives a broken image, which is worse than the wrong one.
#DRIPDROP_BRAND_LOGO=/static/inboxslide_logo.png?v=1

# The long-cadence, low-touch sequence type. "Slow Drip" on Arena.
DRIPDROP_TERM_NURTURE=Nurture
# The daily work queue. "Today's Drip" on Arena.
DRIPDROP_TERM_TODAY="Today's Send"
# The generic noun for one outreach sequence — "keep the drip going". Distinct
# from the named type above.
DRIPDROP_TERM_DRIP=sequence

# These are DERIVED from the three above (plural, lower, sentence case, upper,
# spaceless) and normally need no setting. Override only if a term's plural is
# not just "+s", or its spaceless contraction reads badly.
#DRIPDROP_TERM_NURTURE_PLURAL=
#DRIPDROP_TERM_NURTURE_COMPACT=
#DRIPDROP_TERM_DRIP_PLURAL=

# The dashboard greeting rotation. The built-in list is one sustained water
# metaphor ("Stay wet, {name}.") so it cannot be half-translated a line at a
# time — either replace the whole set or keep Arena's. "|"-separated, {name}
# is the user's first name. Empty/unset keeps the built-in list.
DRIPDROP_GREETINGS=

# ── Theme ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "key:hex" pairs, comma separated. Only keys the stylesheet already reads are
# honoured; unknown keys are ignored rather than raising, so a typo cannot stop
# the app booting. These feed the --dd-* CSS custom properties in
# inject_styles(), so overriding a key recolours every surface using it.
#
# The leading "#" is OPTIONAL and is left off here on purpose: systemd and
# python-dotenv disagree about when "#" starts a trailing comment, and the
# whole format is hex. _env_palette() puts the "#" back.
#
# Brand palette: forest green #063D2F, warm ivory #FEFDF6. In dark, `surface`
# is the exact forest green and `text_l` the exact ivory; in light they swap.
# Every foreground was checked for WCAG contrast ≥ 3.0 against all four ground
# keys (bg, surface, card, card_h), and ≥ 4.5 for body text. If you change one,
# re-check it — several of these are lightened from the obvious choice
# precisely to clear that floor.
DRIPDROP_THEME_DARK=bg:052F24,surface:063D2F,card:0A4A39,card_h:0F5A45,border:14654E,teal:3FD9A8,teal_dim:0B3B2E,muted:9CC0B1,text:D9E8E0,text_l:FEFDF6,warn:F5A524,danger:FCA5A5,good:4ADE80,indigo:A5B4FC,indigo_dim:1E2A52,email_col:3EBFD9,call:3FD9A8,li:A5B4FC,call_col:EF9F27,sms_col:F9A8D4,task_col:3ED9A0
DRIPDROP_THEME_LIGHT=bg:FEFDF6,surface:FFFFFF,card:FFFFFF,card_h:F5F2E4,border:DCD8C4,teal:0B6B52,teal_dim:E4F2EC,muted:5C6B62,text:1F2D26,text_l:063D2F,warn:B45309,danger:DC2626,good:0F7A54,indigo:4F46E5,indigo_dim:EEF0FF,email_col:0E7490,call:0B6B52,li:4F46E5,call_col:B45309,sms_col:BE185D,task_col:15803D

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Newsletter stock imagery. Unset just disables the image picker.
#UNSPLASH_ACCESS_KEY=
